WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.640
Something you might know if you've seen any
of my videos is that I find it interesting

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.420
to take a look at the different exams that
people have to sit around the world and for

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.130
various subjects.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:16.250
So when one particular exam was in the news
recently, I was intrigued to take a look at

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.250
it.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:23.060
Last week, Kim Kardashian revealed that she
had failed her Baby Bar Exam, something taken

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.850
by first year law students in California before
they're able to go on and study for three

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:35.180
more years to eventually take the big California
Bar Exam, which would qualify them to practice

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.180
as an attorney.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.320
The fact that Kim is even studying law may
be news to you.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.320
That's okay.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:47.500
But I found this story and this exam to actually
be pretty interesting.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.360
In short, Kim has been trying to use her massive
public profile for good and help people who

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.960
have been done wrong by America's criminal
justice system.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.790
She met with Donald Trump and was successful
in getting him to grant clemency to several

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:10.700
people who she had fought for, including Alice
Johnson, who had already served 21 years of

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.039
her life sentence, given to her for a nonviolent
first time drug offence.

00:01:17.039 --> 00:01:23.329
Kim has been working alongside human rights
attorneys to help reform aspects of America's

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:24.420
prison system.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:31.109
But now she wants to be more than just a celebrity
attached to the work and she wants to be a

00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:32.799
qualified attorney herself.

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:37.770
Kim said that the best way to change the system
is to know the system.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:43.259
The traditional way to become a lawyer is
to go to law school for three years after

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:45.319
a four year bachelor's degree.

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:50.929
But Kim is doing it a slightly different way
that is similar to an apprenticeship.

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:56.340
She is working with an attorney 18 hours a
week for four years before she'll be able

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.600
to sit the final California bar exam to become
qualified.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.039
After the first year of this she's able to
sit the Baby Bar exam to prove that she's

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:08.349
on the right track.

00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:11.189
But this has become a bit of a hurdle.

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:16.110
The Baby Bar is offered twice per year, and
I've been able to figure out that Kim would

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:22.370
have sat the June 2020 exam, which means that
these here are the actual questions that Kim

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.360
would have got on her exam, and they're from
the State Bar website.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.150
So let's have a look at them.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:33.880
The exam goes for seven hours and consists
of essays and multiple choice questions.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.130
These are some of the essay questions.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:41.480
The first one is about torts law, which is
when someone commits a wrong against another

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.480
person.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:48.010
It says Mel was late for an important meeting
and was driving at least 35 miles an hour

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.720
on a residential road.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.879
The speed limit on this road was 30 miles
per hour.

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:55.920
As Mel rounded a curve in the road.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.459
Nigel suddenly backed out of his driveway
in front of Mel.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:05.019
Mel's headlights were on and his lights would
have been visible if a driver had looked carefully

00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:07.500
to avoid hitting Nigel's car.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.010
Mel both braked hard and turned into the centre
of the street, crossing a yellow line and

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.730
partially entering the lane of oncoming traffic.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.939
Otto was driving towards Mel while adjusting
his car radio.

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:24.340
As a result, Otto did not see Mel in time.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.739
Had he been attentive, he likely could have
avoided an accident.

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:35.269
Instead, the two cars collided, left the road
and ploughed into Penny, who was walking on

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:37.150
the sidewalk.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.790
Nigel's car was not touched.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:40.909
Penny was seriously injured.

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:47.980
Then the question asks what claims Penny can
make against Mel, Otto and Nigel.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:49.709
Now I don't have any background in law.

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:54.159
But we can take a look at one of the examples
solutions that scored highly.

00:03:54.159 --> 00:03:58.709
It's actually pretty in depth and goes through
all of the possible charges for everyone involved.

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:01.900
Here it's starting with Penny verse Mel.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:07.379
It's looking at whether Mel has been negligent
or breached his duty to act as a reasonably

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:08.379
prudent person.

00:04:08.379 --> 00:04:15.310
There is a need to establish actual and proximate
causation and to assess any defences that

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.330
Mel might be able to use.

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:23.600
Then the same thing over again for Penny verse
Otto, where Otto may argue that glancing at

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.660
the radio every now and again doesn't count
as unreasonable behaviour and who actually

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:32.480
caused the accident will again come into dispute.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.440
And then for Penny verse Nigel, the one who
backed out of his driveway.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:41.420
It is likely that Nigel will be found to have
been in breach of his duty to act reasonably

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:45.110
given that he hadn't looked carefully when
he was backing out.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:51.040
In conclusion, Penny will likely be successful
in a negligence claim against Nigel but will

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.790
most likely fail in her claims of negligence
against Mel and Otto.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:59.890
It of course wouldn't be enough to just state
a conclusion of the case.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:06.090
But rather to argue every point for and against
each person and really show an understanding

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.110
of applying all the legal principles.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:16.730
Question two is about contract law and regards
a buyer who purchased 10 $5,000 guitars for

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:22.790
$50,000, and agreed that any complaints about
the condition or quality of the guitars must

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:26.860
be made within 10 days of delivery, and that
payment is due on delivery.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.550
However, the buyer did not pay the invoice.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:35.190
And two weeks after the instruments were delivered,
found that none of their customers wanted

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.920
to buy them in his store, and so sent them
back.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.040
The seller sued the buyer for breach of contract,
and we're asked what arguments the seller

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.140
could make in support of her claim, and what
defences the buyer may use.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.000
Also, what would be the likely outcome?

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:57.410
So then, in this example, answer, we've got
a few pages of going through all the definitions

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:03.080
and things that were agreed to in this contract,
establishing that we did have a valid contract,

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:08.140
and what terms it was reasonable to expect
the buyer and seller to abide by.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:13.750
It might have been okay for the buyer to return
the guitars without paying for them if the

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:18.960
guitars had not been conforming in quality
or some other means, but just because the

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.990
guitars weren't selling in the store does
not speak to any nonconformity of the guitars.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:30.590
And so the seller is not in breach, and the
contract expressly required that the buyer

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:35.280
make this determination within 10 days of
delivery, not 14.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.450
So in conclusion, the court would find that
the buyer was in breach of the contract, and

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:43.830
would be liable to seller for $50,000.

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:49.120
This third question is a real doozy and it
starts off pretty intense.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:55.470
Doug decided to kill Bob, the owner of a small
grocery store because Bob had twice falsely

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.540
accused Doug of shoplifting.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.550
It only gets more and more intense from here.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.420
This question is an example of criminal law.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:09.310
Doug went into Bob's store and killed Bob
by shooting him twice with a handgun.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:14.840
After the shooting Doug notice the cash register,
went over and opened it and took all of the

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:15.840
money out.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.870
Doug then saw a customer Sally, hiding behind
some shelves.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:26.060
He pointed the gun at Sally, took her purse
and tied her hands behind her back.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:31.380
Doug then heard police sirens and ordered
Sally at gunpoint out of the store and into

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:32.970
his car.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:38.900
Officer Fran, who was responding to a report
of gunshots at Bob's store, saw Doug speed

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.310
out of the parking lot in his car.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:46.420
Officer Fran chased him for several blocks
until Doug pulled into an empty parking lot.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:53.690
When he got out of his car Doug fired several
shots at Officer Fran, who responded by firing

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.400
her gun at Doug.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:01.210
She missed Doug, but one of her bullets went
through the window of the car and hit Sally,

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:02.460
killing her.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.320
As Doug tried to run away.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.750
Officer Fran tackled him and knocked the gun
out of his hand.

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:11.180
Doug got up and punched Officer Fran with
his fist.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:14.270
Officer Fran then subdued and arrested Doug.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:15.270
Heck.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.940
With what crimes can Doug reasonably be charged?

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:23.490
What defences if any, may he reasonably raise
and what is the likely result?

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:28.450
Going through the charges here we've got the
murder of Bob, first of all, the state will

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:33.660
be able to show that Doug was the cause of
Bob's death, and they'll be able to show that

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:38.270
there was malice and intent, then they'll
need to decide whether they want to charge

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:42.040
Doug with murder one, murder two or voluntary
manslaughter.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:49.590
For murder one, they would need to show that
Doug murdered Bob with premeditation and deliberation

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:54.690
and for that the state is likely to charge
Doug with murder one and for him to be convicted.

00:08:54.690 --> 00:09:00.210
Then there are all the other crimes, there's
a likely charge for the robbery of Bob's money

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.330
from the store.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:08.810
There's also the robbery of Sally's purse,
the assault of Sally and the battery of Sally,

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:15.770
continuing we have the false imprisonment
of Sally, the kidnapping of Sally, the assault

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:21.010
of Officer Fran and then they'll need to consider
the murder of Sally as well.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:24.920
The state will need to go through the same
analysis that they did with the murder of

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:25.920
Bob.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.430
But then who would be charged with this?

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:29.080
Would it be Doug as well?

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.940
Or would it be Officer Fran since it was a
bullet from her gun?

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:38.190
Well, apparently that depends on which version
of the law certain courts follow in certain

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.430
areas.

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:43.950
If the courts follow the common law, the state
will be able to show that Doug did murder

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:47.460
in the first degree Sally under the felony
murder rule.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:52.790
But if that can't be shown then the state
may have to consider charging Doug with involuntary

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:55.190
manslaughter for Sally's death.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:58.900
Actually in neither of these cases is Officer
Fran charged.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:02.730
And then the last charge for Doug would be
the battery of Officer Fran.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:06.960
And there's a fourth question here that we
can just take a quick glance at.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.010
It's another torts question.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:14.060
It's about someone who does some work on their
land that also affects their neighbours vacant

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:15.060
lot.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:19.420
Even though the neighbour is away for a year,
they chop down the neighbor's tree and instal

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:22.300
a septic system which infringes on their property.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:27.490
When the neighbour gets back, they're angry
about this and decide to sue for the damages.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:33.130
A spoiler here is that despite the involvement
of an innocent contractor, who actually is

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:37.650
the one who did the work, it is the guy who
ordered the work to be done who would remain

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:41.820
directly liable for the tortious conduct he
exhibited.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.760
The maximum score is 800.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.070
And to pass you need 70%, which is 560.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:51.730
Kim's scored 474.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:57.490
And she said she studied for six weeks 10
to 12 hours per day for the exam, which is

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.380
a solid effort.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:04.290
Kim was clearly disheartened and upset in
the announcement of her results.

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:10.630
But really, Kim hasn't done that badly, especially
when you consider the passing rate for her

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:11.630
exam.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:19.990
For first time takers in June 2020 it was
only 27.6% meaning that most people need to

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:22.160
retake this exam.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:27.400
And from what I can tell the way that Kim
is pursuing her studies isn't any easier than

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.020
the conventional path.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:34.580
Having to learn a lot of these concepts through
largely self-led reading may be harder in

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:39.600
some aspects than being taught by professors
and surrounded by peers.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.220
I have nothing bad to say about Kim.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:47.360
I respect that she is trying to better herself
and is open to sharing her failures along

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:48.360
the way.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:53.940
I do hope that she passes her next attempt
at the exam, which may have been the November

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:57.500
2020 one, or the upcoming June 2021 exam.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:59.380
Thanks for watching this video.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:06.220
I have a whole playlist full of other exam
videos, although mostly physics and math ones.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:10.779
Thanks to my Patreon supporters for making
these videos possible.

00:12:10.779 --> 00:12:12.430
And a special shout out to today's Patreon
cat of the day Krishna.

